\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {B1}{\ignorespaces Comparison of four sites (rows) between historical and recent orthophotos (first two columns), the difference between the mapped $2020$--$2025$ and $2000$--$2005$ mean cover of the indicated leaf type (third column), and the annual LTC trajectories averaged over the delineated area (fourth column). Sites id 1 and id 3 show progressive coniferous encroachment on formerly open land, reproduced by the maps as a gradual CON increase from about 2000 onwards at the expense of DEC. Site id 6 shows a coniferous stand cleared between the 2008 and 2023 orthophotos, which appears in the time series as an abrupt CON drop in the early 2010s taken over by the deciduous signal. Sites id 1 (before the encroachment) and id 4 illustrate the limitation of the forest mask: grassland, clear-cuts and sparsely treed pixels are also mapped, and their herbaceous signal is assigned predominantly to DEC.\relax }}{35}{figure.caption.15}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:orthophoto_comparison}{{B1}{35}{Comparison of four sites (rows) between historical and recent orthophotos (first two columns), the difference between the mapped $2020$--$2025$ and $2000$--$2005$ mean cover of the indicated leaf type (third column), and the annual LTC trajectories averaged over the delineated area (fourth column). Sites id 1 and id 3 show progressive coniferous encroachment on formerly open land, reproduced by the maps as a gradual CON increase from about 2000 onwards at the expense of DEC. Site id 6 shows a coniferous stand cleared between the 2008 and 2023 orthophotos, which appears in the time series as an abrupt CON drop in the early 2010s taken over by the deciduous signal. Sites id 1 (before the encroachment) and id 4 illustrate the limitation of the forest mask: grassland, clear-cuts and sparsely treed pixels are also mapped, and their herbaceous signal is assigned predominantly to DEC.\relax }{figure.caption.15}{}}
